-- qemu_deadline.machine -- the machine that gives a world its time.
--
-- Two cores. On the second, two worlds are RESERVED: every 1000 us the
-- kernel runs `control` for 200 of them, done within 500, and `second`
-- for 700, done within the period -- 90 % of the core between them,
-- which is exactly what the kernel gives reservations of a core: it
-- admits 95 and holds 5 of them for the ordinary tasks' own server, in
-- the same ledger, which the first boot of this machine found when the
-- second world asked for the 95th. A third world reserves nothing, and
-- is where every such world is: on core 0, with PID 1.
--
-- Each world asks the kernel, from inside itself, what it runs under
-- (`harb reserved`), and the answers should form a table that only the
-- declarations above them explain:
--
--                   policy          runtime / deadline / period   cores
--   control   SCHED_DEADLINE   200 us / 500 us / 1000 us      core 1 and no other
--   second    SCHED_DEADLINE   700 us / 1000 us / 1000 us     core 1 and no other
--   loose     no reservation   --                             1 core
--
-- The last cell is the partition seen from outside it: the machine has
-- two cores and a world that reserved nothing may run on one of them.
-- And `second` runs as an ordinary user, which the kernel refuses any
-- real-time policy to: it holds a reservation it could not have taken,
-- because the floor gave it before the world became that user.
--
-- Nothing here measures how LATE a world was: the emulator is a
-- program on somebody's host and its time is that host's. What it shows
-- is the MECHANISM -- a core made a partition, a reservation the kernel
-- admitted and reads back as given, and what it did to the worlds that
-- have none. The latency is a board's to show, with a witness that is
-- the next rung's (doc/REALTIME.md).
--
-- `control` stays, so that `second` asks for its time BESIDE a
-- reservation the kernel already holds, and `second` waits for its word
-- (READY) so that the order of the asking is a fact and not a race.
--
-- `second` also declares no `process`, so it runs alone in a process
-- table of its own, behind a stand-in process the floor forks for it --
-- and it is still reserved, because the reservation is taken in the
-- process that execs the world, not in the one that waits for it
-- (SAFE-1). Taken a fork earlier, as it was before that seat, the
-- stand-in held the time and the world ran with none, under a boot line
-- that said otherwise; this machine is where that is judged.
--
-- And each reserved world reads back one more word of the kernel's: it
-- is SIGNALLED if it runs past its runtime (SAFE-1). The floor holds
-- that word until the world listens for it; these two never do, and
-- never run past their time -- `control` sleeps, `second` prints and
-- leaves. The neighbour of `qemu_tick` is the world that listens.

DEFINE MACHINE qemu_deadline AS (
  PROFILE hosted,
  ARCH x86_64,
  KERNEL linux,
  CONSOLE "/dev/ttyS0",
  CORES 2
) RATIONALE "The smallest machine that shows a reservation: two cores, because core 0 is the machine's and a reserved world needs one of its own"

DEFINE CAPABILITY process AS (
  GRANT yes
) RATIONALE "The machine may start worlds; the reserved ones never start another, and need not"

DEFINE USER worker AS (
  UID 2000
) RATIONALE "An identity that could never take a reservation for itself -- the kernel refuses a real-time policy to an ordinary user -- and is given one by the floor before it becomes that user"

DEFINE SERVICE control AS (
  RUN ["/harb", "reserved", "--stay", "/run/control.ready"],
  RESTART always,
  READY "/run/control.ready",
  PERIOD 1000,
  DEADLINE 500,
  RUNTIME 200,
  CORE 1,
  NEEDS [process]
) RATIONALE "A control loop's shape: a short runtime early in every period. It says what it runs under, marks itself ready, and stays, so that the next reservation is admitted beside one the kernel holds"

DEFINE SERVICE second AS (
  RUN ["/harb", "reserved"],
  RESTART never,
  AFTER [control],
  USER worker,
  PERIOD 1000,
  DEADLINE 1000,
  RUNTIME 700,
  CORE 1
) RATIONALE "The rest of the core: with control's 200 it asks 900 of every 1000 us, which is all the kernel gives. It asks after control is ready, so the kernel admits it beside a reservation it already holds -- and it runs as an ordinary user, who is given the reservation and could not have taken it. It declares no process, so it runs alone in a table of its own behind a stand-in, and the reservation is still its own: taken in the process that execs, not in the one that waits"

DEFINE SERVICE loose AS (
  RUN ["/harb", "reserved"],
  RESTART never,
  AFTER [second],
  NEEDS [process]
) RATIONALE "A world that reserved nothing, asked the same question: it runs under the ordinary policy on the one core left to such worlds, which is the partition seen from the outside"
